steps.body:
`Arrays.sort` sorts an array in place. On an `int[]` it only sorts ascending, and `Arrays.sort(a, from, to)` sorts just the indexes from `from` up to `to`, exclusive:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

int[] a = {5, 2, 9, 1};
Arrays.sort(a);
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(a)); // [1, 2, 5, 9]
```

There is no comparator sort for an `int[]`, so `Arrays.sort(a, Collections.reverseOrder())` does not compile. A comparator needs objects, so box the values into an `Integer[]` first. `boxed()` from the Streams lesson turns each `int` into an `Integer`, and `toArray(Integer[]::new)` collects them into a new `Integer[]`:

```java harness
int[] a = {1, 2, 5, 9};
{{code}}
```

```java
Integer[] boxed = Arrays.stream(a).boxed().toArray(Integer[]::new);
Arrays.sort(boxed, Collections.reverseOrder());
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(boxed)); // [9, 5, 2, 1]
```

Sorting a word's letters gives a key that all its anagrams share. Sort the `char[]` from `toCharArray()`, then turn it back into a `String` with `new String(letters)`, so `listen` and `silent` both become `eilnst`.

example.code:
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int[] scores = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            scores[i] = sc.nextInt();
        }
        Arrays.sort(scores);
        System.out.println("Ascending: " + Arrays.toString(scores));

        Integer[] boxed = Arrays.stream(scores).boxed().toArray(Integer[]::new);
        Arrays.sort(boxed, Collections.reverseOrder());
        System.out.println("Descending: " + Arrays.toString(boxed));
        System.out.println("Top: " + boxed[0]);

        String first = sc.next();
        String second = sc.next();
        boolean same = key(first).equals(key(second));
        System.out.println(first + " " + second + " anagrams: " + same);
    }

    static String key(String word) {
        char[] letters = word.toCharArray();
        Arrays.sort(letters);
        return new String(letters);
    }
}

