steps.body:
Each row of an `int[][]` is an `int[]`, so `Arrays.sort` on a grid moves whole rows, and your comparator picks the column to compare. For intervals stored as `{start, end}`, sort by the start:

```java
int[][] meetings = {{9, 10}, {1, 3}, {2, 6}};
Arrays.sort(meetings, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));
System.out.println(Arrays.deepToString(meetings)); // [[1, 3], [2, 6], [9, 10]]
```

Use `Integer.compare`, never `a[0] - b[0]`, which overflows when the values are far apart. `Comparator.comparingInt(a -> a[0])` gives the same order.

After the sort, intervals that overlap sit next to each other. To merge them, keep a list of merged intervals and only ever look at its last one. If the next interval starts after that one ends, it begins a new interval. Otherwise it overlaps, so stretch the last end to whichever end is later:

```java harness
int[][] intervals = {{1, 3}, {2, 6}, {8, 10}};
{{code}}
for (int[] m : merged) System.out.println(m[0] + " " + m[1]);
```

```java
List<int[]> merged = new ArrayList<>();
for (int[] cur : intervals) {
    if (merged.isEmpty() || merged.get(merged.size() - 1)[1] < cur[0]) {
        merged.add(cur);                             // a new interval
    } else {
        int[] last = merged.get(merged.size() - 1);
        last[1] = Math.max(last[1], cur[1]);         // stretch the last one
    }
}
```

Comparing with `<` means intervals that only touch, like `[1, 3]` and `[3, 5]`, merge into one. Print an `int[]` from the list by its `[0]` and `[1]`, never with `println` directly, which shows an address.

example.code:
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int[][] intervals = new int[n][2];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            intervals[i][0] = sc.nextInt();
            intervals[i][1] = sc.nextInt();
        }
        Arrays.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));
        System.out.println("Sorted: " + Arrays.deepToString(intervals));

        List<int[]> merged = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int[] cur : intervals) {
            if (merged.isEmpty() || merged.get(merged.size() - 1)[1] < cur[0]) {
                merged.add(cur);
            } else {
                int[] last = merged.get(merged.size() - 1);
                last[1] = Math.max(last[1], cur[1]);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Merged:");
        for (int[] m : merged) {
            System.out.println(m[0] + " " + m[1]);
        }
    }
}

