            busy += b[1] - b[0];
        }
        System.out.println("Busy: " + busy);
    }
}

steps.body:
A comparator doesn't have to compare one key. It can compute any order, as long as it is consistent: negative when `a` goes first, positive when `b` goes first, and 0 for a tie.

To arrange numbers into the largest number, compare two pieces by gluing them both ways. `9` goes before `34` because `934` beats `349`:

```java
String[] parts = {"3", "30", "34", "5", "9"};
Arrays.sort(parts, (x, y) -> (y + x).compareTo(x + y));
System.out.println(String.join("", parts)); // 9534330
```

Both glued strings have the same length, so `compareTo`, which compares strings character by character, compares them as numbers too. Putting `y + x` first makes the bigger combination win. One edge case: if the first piece is `0`, every piece is, and the answer is `0`, not `000`.

A comparator can also look values up somewhere else. To list positions in score order without moving the scores, sort an `Integer[]` of indexes by the values they point to:

```java
int[] scores = {70, 95, 80};
Integer[] order = {0, 1, 2};
Arrays.sort(order, (i, j) -> Integer.compare(scores[j], scores[i]));
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(order)); // [1, 2, 0], best score first
```

The lambda can read `scores` because it is never reassigned. Sorting objects is **stable**: indexes whose values tie keep their original order.

example.code:
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        String[] pieces = new String[n];
        int[] values = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            pieces[i] = sc.next();
            values[i] = Integer.parseInt(pieces[i]);
        }

        Arrays.sort(pieces, (x, y) -> (y + x).compareTo(x + y));
        String largest = String.join("", pieces);
        if (largest.startsWith("0")) {
            largest = "0";
        }
        System.out.println("Largest: " + largest);

        Integer[] order = new Integer[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            order[i] = i;
        }
        Arrays.sort(order, (i, j) -> Integer.compare(values[j], values[i]));
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("Order:");
        for (int i : order) {
            sb.append(' ').append(i + 1);
        }
        System.out.println(sb);
    }
}

